readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>Doth ''all'' that haunts the waste and wild
Mourn, knowing it will go along with me?</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Passing of Arthur.}}}}

'''2.''' The whole number; every individual or particular, taken collectively; especially, all men or all people: in a plural sense.

{{smaller block|That whelpes are blinde nine dayes, and then begin to see, is the common opinion of ''all''; and some will be apt to descend to oathes upon it. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>And, poured round ''all'',
Old Ocean’s gray and melancholy waste.</poem>
{{right|''Bryant'', Thanatopsis.}}}}

{{smaller|''All'', in either of the preceding uses, is often followed by a limiting phrase with ''of''.}}

{{smaller block|<poem>’Tis not the whole of life to live,
Nor ''all of'' death to die.</poem>
{{right|''Montgomery'', Hymn.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>For ''all of'' wonderful and wild
Had rapture for the lonely child.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L. M., vi. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Then I and you and ''all of'' us fell down.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., iii. 2.}}}}

'''3.''' Everything: as, is that ''all?'' that is ''all''.

{{smaller block|<poem>What though the field be lost?
''All'' is not lost.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 105.}}}}

'''Above all.''' See ''above''.—'''After all,''' after everything has been considered; in spite of everything to the contrary; nevertheless.

{{smaller block|Upon my soul, the women are the best judges ''after all.''
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Critic, i. 1.}}}}

'''All and singular,''' collectively and individually; one and all; all without exception: a common legal phrase.—'''All and some.''' [< ME. ''alle and some'', prop. pl., equiv. to L. ''universi et singuli'', but also used in sing. form ''al and sum'' as adv., altogether: see ''some''.] (''a'') All and sundry; one and all. [Obsolete or archaic.]

{{smaller block|<poem>We are betrayd and ynome [taken],
Horse and harness, lords, ''all and some.''</poem>
{{right|''Rich. C. de L.'', l. 2283.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|Stop your noses, readers, ''all and some.''
{{right|''Dryden'', Abs. and Achit., ii.}}}}
(''b''†) Altogether; wholly.
{{smaller block|<poem>The tale ys wrytyn ''al and sum''
In a boke of Vitas Patrum.</poem>
{{right|''Rob. of Brunne'', Handlyng Synne, l. 169.}}}}

'''All but''', everything but; everything short of; almost; very nearly: as, she is ''all but'' nine years of age.
{{smaller block|<poem>Hold her a wealthy bride within thine arms,
Or ''all but'' hold, and then — cast her aside.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}

'''All in all''' (as noun, ''all-in-all''), all things in all respects; all or everything together; adverbially, altogether.
{{smaller block|That God may be ''all in all.'' {{right|1 Cor. xv. 28.}}
In London she buyes her head, her face, her fashion. O London, thou art her Paradise, her heaven, her ''all-in-all.''
{{right|''Tuke'', On Painting (1616), p. 60. (''Halliwell.'')}}
<poem>Take him for ''all in all'',
I shall not look upon his like again.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 2.}}
<poem>''Acres.'' Dress does make a difference, David.
''Dav.'' ’Tis ''all in all'', I think.</poem>
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, iii. 4.}}
Her good Philip was her ''all-in-all.''
{{right|''Tennyson'', Enoch Arden.}}}}

'''And all''', and everything; and everything else: used in summing up after an enumeration of particulars.
{{smaller block|The first blast of wind laid it [the tree] flat upon the ground, nest, eagles, ''and all.'' {{right|''L’Estrange.''}}
Woo’d and married ''an’ a’.'' {{right|''Burns.''}}}}

'''And all that''', and all the rest of it: used like the preceding, but generally in a slighting or contemptuous way: as, he believes in slate-writing, materialization, ''and all that.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Snuff, or the fan, supply each pause of chat,
With singing, laughing, ogling, ''and all that.''</poem>}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., iii. 17.}}}}

¶
'''At all.''' [< ME. ''at alle.''] (''a''†) In every way; altogether; wholly.
{{smaller block|She is a shrewe ''at al.'' {{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Merchant’s Tale.}}}}
(''b'') In any degree; in any degree whatever; in the least degree; for any reason; on any consideration: as, I was surprised at his coming ''at all.''
{{smaller block|Thirdly, the starres have not onely varied their longitudes, whereby their ascents are altered; but have also changed their declinations, whereby their rising ''at all,'' that is, their appearing, hath varied.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err.}}}}
(''c'') In any way; to any extent; of any kind or character: in negative, interrogative, or conditional clauses (compare I., 4): as, he was not ''at all'' disturbed; did you hear anything ''at all?'' if you hear anything ''at all'', let me know; no offense ''at all.''
{{smaller block|An if this be ''at all.'' {{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, v. 1.}}}}
'''Before all,''' before everything; before everything else; beyond all.—'''Beyond all,''' beyond everything; beyond everything else; above all.—'''For all.''' (''a'') For all purposes, occasions, or times: especially in the phrases ''once for all'' and ''for good and all.'' [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Learn now, ''for all'',
. . . I care not for you.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', Cymbeline, ii. 3.}}}}
(''b'') Notwithstanding; in spite of (the thing or fact mentioned): followed by an object noun or pronoun or an object clause with ''that'', which is often omitted: as, ''for all that'', the fact remains the same; you may do so ''for all'' (''that'') I care, or ''for all'' me. See ''for.''
{{smaller block|Go, sirrah; ''for all'' you are my man, go wait upon my cousin Shallow. {{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>As Noah’s pigeon, which return’d no more,
Did show, she footing found, ''for all'' the flood.</poem>{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Immortal. of Soul, xxxii.}}}}
{{smaller block|A man’s a man ''for a’'' that. {{right|''Burns'', For A’ That.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
'''In all.''' {{smaller|(''a'') In the whole number; all included: as, there were ''in all'' at least a hundred persons present.}}
{{smaller block|In this tyme had Steuen regned auht ȝere ''in alle.''
{{right|''Rob. of Brunne'', Langtoft’s Chron. (ed. Hearne), p. 122.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') In whole: as, in part or ''in all''.—'''Over all†''', everywhere. ''Chaucer.'' [Now only in its literal meaning.]—'''Two''' (or '''twos''') '''all, three all''', etc., in certain games, means that all (or merely both) the players or sides have two, three, etc., points.—'''When all comes to all''', when everything is explained; at bottom.—'''With all†'''. See ''withal.''}}

'''III.''' ''n.'' [Preceded by an article or a pronoun, rarely with an intervening adjective.] '''1.''' A whole; an entirety; a totality of things or qualities. ''The All'' is used for the universe.
{{smaller block|<poem>And will she yet abase her eyes on me, . . .
On me, whose ''all'' not equals Edward’s moiety?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., i. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' One’s whole interest, concern, or property: usually with a possessive pronoun: as, she has given her ''all.'' [Formerly and still dialectically with pl. ''alls.'']
{{smaller block|Though a very industrious tradesman, I was twice burnt out, and lost my little ''all'' both times.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Critic, i. 2.}}
Old Boreas — we are glad of that — was required to pack up “his ''alls''” and be off. {{right|''De Quincey'', Herodotus, ii.}}}}
[For ''all'' in composition, see the adverb, at end.]

'''all''' (âl), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''al'', rarely ''alle'', < AS. ''eall'', ''eal'' (= OS. ''al'', etc.), prop. neut. acc. (cf. AS. ''ealles'' = OS. ''alles'' = Goth. ''allis'', adv., prop. gen. neut.) of ''eall'', ''eal'', all: see ''all'', ''a.'' The adverbial uses of ''all'' overlap the adjectival uses: see especially under ''all'', ''a.'', I., at end.] '''1.''' Wholly; entirely; completely; altogether; quite. In this use common with adverbs of degree, espe-

readings.c2s2:
cially ''too'': as, he arrived ''all too'' late.
{{smaller block|<poem>And tell us what occasion of import
Hath ''all'' so long detain’d you from your wife.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He held them sixpence ''all too'' dear.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', quoted in Othello, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Alone, alone, ''all'', ''all'' alone,
Alone on a wide, wide sea.</poem>{{right|''Coleridge'', Ancient Mariner.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>O, yet methought I saw the Holy Grail,
''All'' pall’d in crimson samite.</poem>{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}
{{smaller|[From the frequent Middle English use of ''all'' in this sense before verbs with the prefix ''to-'' (see ''to-''<sup>2</sup>, ''to-break'', ''to-cut'', ''to-tear'', etc.), that prefix, when no longer felt as such, came to be attached to the adverb, ''all to'' or ''alto'' being regarded as an adverbial phrase or word, and sometimes improperly used, in later English, with verbs having originally no claim to the prefix.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The sowdan and the cristen euerichone,
Ben ''al to-hewe'' and stiked at the bord.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Man of Law’s Tale, l. 332.}}}}
{{smaller block|And a certain woman cast a piece of a millstone upon Abimelech’s head, and ''all to-brake'' [printed ''all to brake''] his scull.{{right|Judges ix. 53.}}}}
{{smaller block|They . . . were ''alle to-cutte'' with the stones.{{right|''Caxton'', Golden Legend, p. 236.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>She plumes her feathers, and lets grow her wings,
That in the various bustle of resort,
Were ''all to-ruffled'' [sometimes printed ''altoruffled''], and sometimes impair’d.</poem>{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 380.]}}}}

'''2.''' Even; just: at first emphatic or intensive. {{smaller|(''a'') With prepositional phrases of place or time, in later use, particularly in ballad poetry, little more than merely expletive or pleonastic: as, ''all'' in the month of May; ''all'' in the morning tide.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>When ''all'' aloud the wind doth blow.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2 (song).}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>A damsel lay deploring,
''All'' on a rock reclined.</poem>
{{right|''Gay.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>One night my pathway swerving east, I saw
The pelican on the casque of our Sir Bors
''All'' in the middle of the rising moon.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}

(''b'') With conjunctions ''if'' and ''though'', in conditional and concessive clauses: ''If all, though all'', or reversely, ''all if, all though'', even if, even though. These forms are obsolete, except the last, which is now written as one word, ''although'' (which see).
{{smaller block|<poem>I am nought wode, ''alle if'' I lewed be.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iii. 398.}}}}
{{smaller block|''ȝif alle'' it be so that men seyn, that this crowne is of thornes.
{{right|''Mandeville'' (ed. Halliwell), p. 13.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Thof alle'' that he werred in wo & in strife,
The foure & tuenty houres he spended in holy life.</poem>
{{right|''Rob. of Brunne'', Langtoft’s Chron. (ed. Hearne), p. 23.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Alle thoughe'' it be clept a see, it is no see.
{{right|''Mandeville'' (ed. Halliwell), p. 266.}}}}

[When the verb in such clauses, according to a common subjunctive construction, was placed before the subject, the conjunction ''if'' or ''though'' might be omitted, leaving ''all'' as an apparent conjunction, in the sense of even if, although; especially in the formula ''al be'', as ''al be it, al be it that, al be that'' (now ''albe, albeit'', which see).
{{smaller block|<poem>''Al be'' her herte wel nigh to-broke
No word of pride ne grame she spoke.</poem>
{{right|''Lay le Freine'', l. 347, in Weber’s Metr. Rom., I.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Al'' were it that my auncetres were rude
Yit may the highe God . . .
Graunte me grace to lyve vertuously.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Wife of Bath’s Tale, l. 316.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>His sacrifice he dede . . . with alle circumstances
''Al'' telle I nat as now his observances.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Knight’s Tale, l. 1406.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>But living art may not least part expresse, . . .
''All'' were it Zeuxis or Praxiteles,
His dædale hand would faile and greatly faynt.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., iii., Prol.]}}}}
(''c'') With conjunction ''as'': ''All as.'' (1) Just when; when; as.
{{smaller block|<poem>''All as'' his straying flocke he fedde.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., Prol.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He their courtesy to requite,
Gave them a chain of twelve marks weight,
''All as'' he lighted down. {{right|''Scott'', Marmion, i. 11.}}</poem>}}
(2) As if.
{{smaller block|<poem>The kene cold blowes through my beaten hyde,
''All as'' I were through the body gryde.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., Feb.}}}}
'''3†.''' Only; exclusively.
{{smaller block|<poem>I shall never marry like my sisters,
To love my father ''all''. {{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 1.}}</poem>}}

'''All along.''' (''a'') Throughout; continuously; uninterruptedly; from the beginning onward: as, I knew that ''all along''.
{{smaller block|Ishmael . . . went forth, . . . weeping ''all along'' as he went.
{{right|Jer. xli. 6.}}}}
(''b'') From end to end; in ''bookbinding'', (sewed) in such a manner that the thread passes from end to end of each section. (''c'') At full length.
{{smaller block|<poem>I found a woman of a matchless form
Stretch’d ''all along'' upon the marble floor.</poem>
{{right|''Tuke'', Five Hours, ii.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And there in gloom cast himself ''all along''.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Balin and Balan.}}}}

'''All along of.''' See ''along''<sup>2</sup>.—'''All in the wind''' (''naut.''), too close to the wind: said of a vessel so brought up into the wind that the sails shake.—'''All of a sudden,''' suddenly; quite unexpectedly.

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Matters have taken so clever a turn ''all of a sudden'', that I could find it in my heart to be so good-humoured!
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, iv. 2.}}}}

'''All one''', the same thing in effect; quite the same.

{{smaller block|Yet I have the wit to think my master is a kind of a knave: but that’s ''all one'', if he be but one knave.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iii. 1.}}}}

'''All out†''' [ME. ''al oute'', ''alout''], entirely; completely; quite.

{{smaller block|Then come these wikkyde Jewes . . . and brake theyre thees, and slewe them ''alle owte''.
{{right|''MS. Lincoln'' (A), i. 17, folio 184. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Whanne he hadde don his wille ''al oute''.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 2101.}}}}

Used especially with ''drink'' (see ''carouse'').

{{smaller block|I quaught, I drinke ''all out''. {{right|''Palsgrave.''}}}}

{{smaller block|''Allus'' [F.], ''all out''; or a carouse fully drunk up. {{right|''Cotgrave.''}}}}

'''All over.''' (''a'') In every part; everywhere; over the whole body. ''Chaucer.'' (''b'') Thoroughly; entirely: as, “Dombey and Son” is Dickens ''all over''. [Colloq.] (''c'') Indisposed; generally ill; having an all-overish feeling. [Colloq.] (''d'') All past; entirely ceased: as, that is ''all over''.—'''All over with''', done with; finished: as, it is ''all over with'' their friendship; colloquially, the trouble is ''all over with''.

{{smaller block|Ay, a final sentence, indeed!—’tis ''all over with'' you, faith!
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, iv. 3.}}}}

'''All right''', an idiomatic colloquial phrase, either adjectival or adverbial, expressive of satisfaction with, approval of, or assent to anything, and equivalent to quite correct or correctly, satisfactory or satisfactorily, in a satisfactory condition or manner, etc.: as, your conduct or your dress is ''all right''; he has done it ''all right''; “Are you ready? ''All right''; go ahead.”—'''All the''' [''the'', adv.: see ''the''<sup>2</sup>], to all that extent; so much: as, ''all the'' better; ''all the'' fitter; ''all the'' sooner. See ''the''<sup>2</sup>.—'''All there''', up to the mark; wide awake; in strict fashion; first-rate. [Slang.]—'''All up with''', at an end; all over with: as, when the pistol was raised he knew that it was ''all up with'' him. [Colloq.]

[''All'', in composition, sometimes forms a true compound, as in ''almighty'', ''already'', ''always'', ''algates'', but usually stands,
